1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,252
Dakshin Marg — the southern path. An AI-enabled Antarctic sea-ice, iceberg

2
00:00:06,302 --> 00:00:12,366
trajectory and navigation decision support system, built for the Smart India

3
00:00:12,416 --> 00:00:16,790
Hackathon for the Ministry of Earth Sciences and NCPOR.

4
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:26,781
Every austral summer, India's research stations are resupplied through Antarctic sea ice — where the

5
00:00:26,831 --> 00:00:33,062
ice charts are days old, icebergs move, and the ice edge can shift overnight. Dakshin

6
00:00:33,112 --> 00:00:39,123
Marg turns open satellite and model data into a risk picture, route options, and a

7
00:00:39,173 --> 00:00:44,370
re-planning decision — and it keeps the human in command at every step.

8
00:00:51,250 --> 00:00:58,416
This is the running system, in LIVE mode. Everything on screen is computed from

9
00:00:58,466 --> 00:01:07,275
real datasets — NSIDC sea-ice concentration, USNIC iceberg positions, BYU and NIC iceberg tracks,

10
00:01:07,325 --> 00:01:13,760
and Open-Meteo wind and temperature. No API keys, and no invented data.

11
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:28,917
Before the deep dive, a walk through the whole console. Plan: Mission Control, the Route Planner with

12
00:01:28,967 --> 00:01:37,147
its Pareto trade-offs, and Vessel Profiles, where each ice class carries its own capability limits. There are

13
00:01:37,197 --> 00:01:43,490
no mock screens here — every panel is the real application reading the real engines.

14
00:01:50,450 --> 00:01:59,357
Environment: sea-ice concentration and forecast, iceberg intelligence, weather and ocean, and the risk picture. Every

15
00:01:59,407 --> 00:02:05,098
layer declares what it is and how old it is — observation, model output, or

16
00:02:05,148 --> 00:02:12,294
planned. Ocean currents and waves are marked PLANNED, because no data source is connected yet:

17
00:02:12,344 --> 00:02:17,500
the system says so instead of drawing something that does not exist.

18
00:02:19,050 --> 00:02:28,232
Operations: what-if scenarios, verification and hindsight skill scores, data provenance with a lineage for every product,

19
00:02:28,282 --> 00:02:34,107
the alert centre, and system health. This is the audit trail — if a number is

20
00:02:34,157 --> 00:02:39,830
challenged in a review, this is where you find exactly how it was produced.

21
00:02:47,130 --> 00:02:52,261
This is the latest NSIDC daily analysis in our data window — the first

22
00:02:52,311 --> 00:02:59,291
of September, mean concentration thirty-nine point eight percent along the corridor. I can step

23
00:02:59,341 --> 00:03:06,692
back through eight observation days, or forecast forward. At plus forty-eight hours the damped-trend

24
00:03:06,742 --> 00:03:13,131
model gives forty-two percent, with an uncertainty band of five point seven percent and

25
00:03:13,181 --> 00:03:18,903
a backtest error of four percent — against four point two for persistence. The

26
00:03:18,953 --> 00:03:25,120
badge changes to SEA-ICE FORECAST, because this is model output, not an observation.

27
00:03:31,910 --> 00:03:39,017
Thirteen icebergs are charted in this area; seventeen have real tracks from the BYU database,

28
00:03:39,067 --> 00:03:45,943
and five are classified as moving. The drift forecast carries an empirical P90 corridor of

29
00:03:45,993 --> 00:03:52,945
about forty-two kilometres at seven days. And the card states openly that the track archive

30
00:03:52,995 --> 00:03:58,640
lags real time — which is why current positions come from the USNIC chart.

31
00:04:00,190 --> 00:04:05,818
Risk is the core product: the IMO POLARIS risk index for sea ice,

32
00:04:05,868 --> 00:04:14,204
Overland's icing predictor, and iceberg hazard zones — combined worst-case, never averaged. With

33
00:04:14,254 --> 00:04:19,794
a PC5 ice class, the worst cell in the area is normal operation:

34
00:04:19,844 --> 00:04:25,123
RIO plus ten. Now watch the only thing I change — the vessel.

35
00:04:25,173 --> 00:04:30,888
PC7: minus nine, and a quarter of the cells move into the elevated

36
00:04:30,938 --> 00:04:36,741
band. No ice class at all: minus forty-nine, and over half the area

37
00:04:36,791 --> 00:04:43,030
becomes critical. Same ocean, same data — the vessel changes the answer.

38
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:51,454
For routing, the optimizer does not invent cost weights — it applies severity ceilings

39
00:04:51,504 --> 00:04:59,424
as hard constraints. DIRECT is eight hundred and sixty-seven nautical miles, sixty-nine hours, with

40
00:04:59,474 --> 00:05:06,428
twenty percent of the route in medium severity. CONSERVATIVE costs a hundred and thirty

41
00:05:06,478 --> 00:05:13,110
extra miles to keep the whole route low. The recommendation cites IMO circular 1519

42
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:20,516
— avoid elevated-risk areas when planning. And fuel reads NOT COMPUTED, because no validated

43
00:05:20,566 --> 00:05:25,910
consumption model exists. We do not guess numbers we cannot defend.

44
00:06:03,660 --> 00:06:11,111
Now the real workflow — a resupply mission. Origin picked on the map, destination Bharati station

45
00:06:11,161 --> 00:06:18,613
in Prydz Bay, departure the second of September. The wizard validates the date against the actual

46
00:06:18,663 --> 00:06:24,954
data window — the twenty-fifth of August to the fourth of September — and tells me

47
00:06:25,004 --> 00:06:31,605
which observation and which weather hour it will use. Ice class PC5, twelve and a half

48
00:06:31,655 --> 00:06:38,410
knots, and a deliberately strict ceiling: nothing worse than low on the remaining route.

49
00:06:52,310 --> 00:06:59,210
The departure date is validated against the real data window before the mission is accepted —

50
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:06,758
no silent substitution. The optimizer then runs on the risk surface resolved for that departure time:

51
00:07:06,808 --> 00:07:13,260
one profile per severity ceiling, each one either a feasible plan or an honest refusal.

52
00:07:14,810 --> 00:07:21,074
Three options for that departure — all eight hundred and sixty-nine miles and low

53
00:07:21,124 --> 00:07:27,154
at this hour. I take the recommended DIRECT line. Acceptance is a human click:

54
00:07:27,204 --> 00:07:33,000
the system never starts a voyage by itself. The vessel then moves along the

55
00:07:33,050 --> 00:07:39,860
accepted geometry at the optimizer's own speed model — there is no decorative animation.

56
00:07:41,410 --> 00:07:47,984
I advance the mission clock twelve hours at a time. Conditions at the vessel update with

57
00:07:48,034 --> 00:07:55,362
the clock — wind, air temperature, nearest ice cell. And every three simulated hours the remaining

58
00:07:55,412 --> 00:08:01,986
route is re-assessed against the data valid at that time. This is where the system earns

59
00:08:02,036 --> 00:08:07,180
its keep: I do not have to notice the change — the system watches it.

60
00:08:13,160 --> 00:08:20,182
There it is — ROUTE REVIEW REQUIRED. The re-assessment found medium severity on the remaining

61
00:08:20,232 --> 00:08:26,645
route, which breaks the ceiling the operator set. It names the primary factor, and it

62
00:08:26,695 --> 00:08:32,652
pauses. It does not reroute on its own. I ask for alternatives from our current

63
00:08:32,702 --> 00:08:40,484
position, at the current time. Three candidates; CONSERVATIVE is recommended — five hundred and seventy

64
00:08:40,534 --> 00:08:46,947
miles, and the smallest exposure of the three. I accept it: the active route changes,

65
00:08:46,997 --> 00:08:53,410
the track restarts from this point, and the decision is written into the mission log.

66
00:08:54,960 --> 00:09:01,358
For the finale, a repeatable drill. It simulates exactly one fact — iceberg D23

67
00:09:01,408 --> 00:09:08,051
ungrounding and drifting a hundred kilometres north, into our corridor — and it is

68
00:09:08,101 --> 00:09:15,969
deterministic: the same document every run. Every route, every risk number, every corridor radius

69
00:09:16,019 --> 00:09:20,540
is computed by the same live engines you have just seen.

70
00:09:22,090 --> 00:09:28,367
Twenty-four hours at cruise speed: three hundred miles covered — then the simulated

71
00:09:28,417 --> 00:09:34,465
re-sighting arrives, and D23 is inside the corridor. The remaining leg goes from

72
00:09:34,515 --> 00:09:39,801
low to critical, with a closest approach of two and a half kilometres.

73
00:09:39,851 --> 00:09:47,348
Re-planning recommends CONSERVATIVE: five hundred and ninety-two miles, all low — twenty-five miles

74
00:09:47,398 --> 00:09:52,760
longer than staying put. I accept it, and the new route becomes active.

75
00:10:24,770 --> 00:10:32,195
One thing on honesty, because it matters for a decision-support system. Everything in this

76
00:10:32,245 --> 00:10:38,674
demo is real open data and real math, with exactly one labelled simulated fact

77
00:10:38,724 --> 00:10:46,149
— the berg re-sighting. The system refuses dates outside the data window, refuses horizons

78
00:10:46,199 --> 00:10:52,960
beyond seventy-two hours, and reports no safe route when there is not one. Dakshin

79
00:10:53,010 --> 00:11:00,020
Marg predicts, assesses and recommends. The officer on the bridge decides. Thank you.
